From efe278f8b4bb16aa17a1297cc2f9cd5c7b884fa0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: auditor <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 23:33:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] research tooling: barrier-empty-input arity,
 negative-quantity gate, corpus unit audit

Credit: an independent audit pass (uploaded as AUDIT_SLICE76.zip) found
these three; all three are re-derived and re-verified from source here,
independently, before being fixed, because "another AI said so" is not
evidence - reproducing the bug is.

1. tools/skill_test.py barrier_r_for_all_bars - empty-input early return
   gave a 2-tuple; every real caller (70+ sites in tools/ and tests/)
   unpacks 3 (`indices, net, bars_used`). Reproduced: a 3-bar series
   raises `ValueError: not enough values to unpack` at the CALLER, which
   looks like a caller bug rather than what it actually is - a corpus
   too short (or an all-NaN ATR window) to produce an eligible bar.
   Fixed to return three empty arrays; the misleading `Tuple[np.ndarray,
   np.ndarray]` return annotation is corrected to 3 elements too.
   tests/test_barrier_empty_input.py, 3 tests.

2. risk_management.gate_order - `if quantity and float(quantity) > 0.0:`
   guards all four size gates (per-trade risk, notional, aggregate
   exposure, correlation). A NEGATIVE quantity is falsy-guarded the same
   as zero and falls through to `RiskDecision(ok=True, reason=
   "APPROVED")` with none of the four gates run. VERIFIED SEVERITY, NOT
   OVERSTATED: the two current call sites in trading_engine.py pass
   0.0 (the pre-gate, size-independent by design and correctly still
   unblocked) or `sizing.qty` (the final gate) - and
   BillionairePositionSizing refuses rather than emit a negative size, so
   this branch is NOT reachable through the engine today. Fixed anyway as
   defense in depth against a future or external caller (a hand-built
   TradeIntent, a different sizer, a test harness) - the zero-quantity
   pre-gate path is preserved exactly. tests/test_negative_quantity_gate.py,
   6 tests, including one that pins the zero-quantity pre-gate behaviour
   does NOT regress.

3. tools/corpus_unit_audit.py (new) - a meaning-layer check the byte-level
   prefix-hash tests cannot provide. VERIFIED by running it against the
   actual shipped corpus: 2026-08-18 through 2026-08-22 carry
   5.4e9..3.5e10 in volume_traded against a 1,476-row median of 227,714 -
   23,738x to 151,759x, for exactly 5 contiguous days, then a clean return
   to the prior order of magnitude. That is quote-notional (USD) leaking
   into a base-volume column, not a real volume day (BTC does not trade
   five billion COINS in a day). CONFIRMED NOT TO REACH the currently
   cleared/forward-shadowed signal: funding_carry_fade_v1 and
   _btc_v1 contain no reference to volume (test pins this). CONFIRMED TO
   REACH seven of the eleven frozen families that DO reference volume
   (btc_alt_spillover_v1, compression_breakout_v1, open_gap_fade_v1,
   post_shock_fade_v1, range_location_fade_v1, sign_flip_momentum_v1,
   ts_momentum_v1) - listed by a living test rather than a one-time note,
   so the list stays current as signal files change.
   tests/test_corpus_unit_audit.py, 6 tests. One of them
   (test_the_known_unit_swap_is_still_present) is written to FAIL the day
   a human corrects the source data, on purpose, so the fix cannot happen
   silently.

What did NOT change: FUND_ABS, join, horizon, barrier geometry, any
threshold, GATE_PATH, the corpus bytes themselves (the unit-swap defect
is documented, not silently patched over - fixing it means re-fetching
those 5 days from source and would change sha256, which needs a human
decision, not a background bug fix).

Verification: full suite 5,006 passed, 2 skipped (baseline 4,953; prior
rounds 4,990; this round adds 16 new tests net of the empty-input fix
also being exercised by 3 pre-existing... see individual counts above).
